{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdXk3TkgnQyVJ7Qg6fEz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Andrew Siemion, who holds the Bernard M. Oliver chair for SETI at the SETI Institute and is principal investigator of Breakthrough Listen, says that what the new agentic observatories find \"may have much more in common with themselves than with the humans who created them.\" He calls the development \"a breakthrough in translation.\" The remark comes from an NVIDIA article about AI agents now running parts of the search at the Allen Telescope Array, and it rests on a hypothesis the article states with qualifications.\n\nThe hypothesis is that some scientists have suggested extraterrestrial civilizations, if they exist, could be billions of years older than humanity and might have developed technologies far beyond ours, including forms of AI. If so, a signal could come from a machine intelligence, and software agents may be well suited to recognizing it. The article offers this as a suggestion, and no such signal has been reported.\n\nWhat the article does document is the tooling. At the array, agents can steer the telescope, enhance and correct incoming data and apply AI models to the live stream from one natural language request, using NVIDIA's Holoscan platform to process the sensor data. The one result it cites is the first real-time AI detection of a pulsar, in the Crab Nebula. That is a known astronomical object, which means the translation idea remains untested against any unknown signal. For now, the case for it rests on Siemion's view of what agentic observatories might find, and the evidence is a pulsar detection."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1723,"cache_read_input_tokens":4149,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1723},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier